{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Ontologies and Data Models"}, {"type": "position", "content": 6}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when talking about ontologies and data models."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand key information about the concepts, principles, and methodologies in simple spoken and written situations. Read and write about programming techniques, use cases, kinds, and sequences in simple spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures.  Writes simple but clear texts describing or reviewing places to visit in the region, using an appropriate range of vocabulary and grammatical structures."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob06re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Modals for Ability, Can, and Could"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use the modal verbs <strong>can</strong> and <strong>could</strong> to talk about skills or capacities."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When we refer to ability, we are talking about two different concepts:<br/><br/>  On the one hand, we have <strong>general ability</strong>, which refers to something that you learned how to do, and you do it whenever you want, like speaking French or swimming.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">He <strong>can</strong> set up a computer.<br/> He <strong>couldn't</strong> set up the computer.</span><br/><br/>  On the other hand, there is <strong>specific ability</strong>, which means something that you can or can't do in a particular situation. For instance, to be able to go somewhere or see something.<br/><br/>  Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">She <strong>can't</strong> find the right language model.<br/>  She <strong>couldn't</strong> find the right language model.</span><br/><br/>  Notice that normally we use <strong>can</strong> to talk about general ability in <strong>the present</strong>, and we use <strong>could</strong> to describe general ability in <strong>the past</strong>. However, for referring to <strong>general ability in the future</strong>, we use the expression <span class=\"italic\">to be able to</span>.<br/><br/>   Examples:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">By December, you <strong>will be able to</strong> code in Java.<br/> You <strong>won't be able to</strong> program in C+ in a weekend! It usually takes months.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I can see why you think that; people confuse those concepts easily."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We can create data models to represent the business reality, providing a design for data storage location and its management."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Now I see why I couldn't differentiate both concepts."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Connectors for Expressing Result, for Reformulating, and for Providing Alternatives "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<strong>Connectors</strong> are words or expressions that link phrases or sentences together. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "By using connectors, we can express relationships between ideas, making our writing easier to read, especially when referring to complex concepts.<br/><br/>  Some connectors express the <strong>result</strong> or <strong>consequence</strong> of an action or situation. Observe:<br/><br/>   <span class=\"italic\">We couldn't finish the presentation. We postponed the presentation.</br> We couldn't finish the presentation, <strong>therefore</strong> we postponed it.</br> We couldn't finish the presentation, <strong>as a result</strong>, we postponed it.</span><br/><br/>  There are connectors that we can use for <strong>reformulating</strong> or further <strong>explaining</strong> a concept or complex idea.<br/><br/>  For example: <br/>   <span class=\"italic\">Debugging is the process of correcting program errors, <strong>in other words</strong>, it is how you repair what is wrong with your code.</span><br/><br/>  Finally, we use certain connectors when we want to <strong>provide a different meaning</strong>, </strong>contrast</strong> or <strong>alternative</strong> to the idea we are expressing.<br/><br/>  For example: <br/>   <span class=\"italic\">Certain automated systems can recognize some particular patterns, <strong>on the other hand</strong>, a normal human brain can effortlessly recognize millions of patterns.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I didn't have to work, so I could study the whole weekend; as a result, I think I am ready."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, I can't understand what ontologies and data models are. In other words, I think they are the same."}, {"type": "example", "content": "On the other hand, we can create data models to represent the business reality, providing a design for data storage location and its management."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "same "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "equal or identical  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In other words, I think they are the same. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob06aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "easily "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "in an easy way, without complications "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: People confuse those concepts easily."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob06aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "concept "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "idea or notion "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Now I see why I couldn't differentiate both concepts."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob06aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "belong "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be a part of something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: An ontology consists of classes to which relevant objects belong, regardless of storage location."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob06aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "entity "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "object or thing that exists in reality "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: SUPPLIER, defined as an ENTITY that provides goods or services."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob06aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "superclass "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "superior category that includes smaller categories or classes  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We can see that the last two classes include as their parent or \u201csuperclass,\u201d the ENTITY class."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n2le03ob06aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look up more information about data models and ontology."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Design a synoptic table in which you explain the concepts of data models and ontology."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Show to your teacher. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I read about data models and ontology."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain data models and ontology."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I differentiate data models and ontology. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob06re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Clara </span>: Hi, Sonia. Did you study for the Artificial Intelligence test? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Sonia </span>: Yes, I did. I didn't have to work, so I could study the whole weekend; as a result, I think I am ready. Do you have any questions? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Clara </span>: Well, I can't understand what ontologies and data models are. In other words, I think they are the same. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Sonia </span>: I can see why you think that; people confuse those concepts easily."}, {"time": "26000", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob06re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Clara </span>: Can you please explain to me the difference between an ontology and a data model? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Sonia </span>: Sure! An ontology is a view of the concepts, relations, and rules for a particular area of business information. This is an abstract view that doesn't take into account how that information can be stored as data; therefore, an ontology consists of classes to which relevant objects belong, regardless of storage location. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Clara </span>: And what about data models? Can you tell me what they are? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Sonia </span>: On the other hand, we can create data models to represent the business reality, providing a design for data storage location and its management."}, {"time": "67000", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob06re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Clara </span>: Now I see why I couldn't differentiate both concepts. Can you give me an example of an ontology? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Sonia </span>: Sure. For instance, a business ontology can include the following classes: ENTITY, defined as a business or person; SUPPLIER, defined as an ENTITY that provides goods or services; Finally, CUSTOMER, defined as an ENTITY that buys the company's goods or services. As an example, we can see that the last two classes include as their parent or \u201csuperclass,\u201d the ENTITY class. In other words, we can say the ENTITY class includes the last two classes. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Clara </span>: Thanks for your help. Now I think I will be able to pass the exam tomorrow."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n2le03ob06re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n2le03ob06re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n2le03ob06re5vsc01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": ""}]}, "type": "video"}], "type": "video", "_data": [], "id": 3, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can read about ontologies and data models."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can write about ontologies and data models to explain what they are."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can understand what ontologies and data models are."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "How does a computer program recognize the difference between two objects or classes?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What is an effective method for establishing classes and subclasses when writing code?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "co